CEFR LEVEL RULES for {{level}}:

{{#if (eq level "A1")}}
📚 VOCABULARY:
- Use ONLY the 500 most common English words
- Avoid phrasal verbs (use "start" not "set off")
- Avoid idioms and figurative language
- Maximum 1 topic-specific word per paragraph (define it immediately in parentheses)
- Avoid abstract nouns (use "happy" not "happiness")

📝 GRAMMAR:
- Present Simple tense ONLY (he walks, she eats)
- No Past tense except for very common verbs (was, had, went)
- No Future tense (avoid "will")
- No Passive voice
- No Conditionals
- No Relative clauses (who, which, that)
- Subject + Verb + Object structure only
- Maximum 1 conjunction per sentence (and, but, or)

📏 SENTENCES:
- 5-8 words per sentence (STRICT)
- One idea per sentence
- No embedded clauses

📖 CONTENT:
- Only concrete, everyday topics
- No metaphors or symbolism
- Describe what can be seen, touched, heard
- Use present-time context when possible

📐 LENGTH: 800-1200 words
📃 PARAGRAPHS: 3-5 short sentences each
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq level "A2")}}
📚 VOCABULARY:
- Use the 1000 most common English words
- Introduce 2-3 topic-specific words per paragraph (define briefly)
- Basic phrasal verbs allowed (look at, get up, come back)
- Avoid complex idioms
- Prefer concrete over abstract words

📝 GRAMMAR:
- Present Simple and Past Simple
- Basic Future (will + verb, going to)
- No Passive voice
- No Conditionals except Type 0 (If water boils, it becomes steam)
- Simple comparatives (bigger, more beautiful)
- Basic connectors: and, but, so, because, then, first, next

📏 SENTENCES:
- 8-12 words per sentence
- Maximum 1 subordinate clause per sentence
- Clear subject-verb-object order

📖 CONTENT:
- Familiar topics (family, hobbies, daily life, basic history)
- Simple cause-effect relationships
- Chronological storytelling

📐 LENGTH: 1000-1250 words
📃 PARAGRAPHS: 4-6 sentences each
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq level "B1")}}
📚 VOCABULARY:
- Use up to 2500 common words
- Technical terms allowed with brief explanation
- Common idioms acceptable (it's raining cats and dogs)
- Basic academic vocabulary

📝 GRAMMAR:
- All simple tenses
- Present Perfect (I have seen)
- Modals (can, could, should, might)
- Conditionals Type 0 and 1
- Basic Relative clauses (who, which, that)
- Connectors: however, although, therefore, moreover

📏 SENTENCES:
- 10-15 words per sentence
- 1-2 subordinate clauses allowed
- Varied sentence openings

📖 CONTENT:
- Abstract concepts with concrete examples
- Opinion expression with reasons
- Compare and contrast structures

📐 LENGTH: 900-1200 words
📃 PARAGRAPHS: 4-6 sentences each
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq level "B2")}}
📚 VOCABULARY:
- Wide range including abstract terms
- Field-specific vocabulary
- Idioms and collocations
- Formal and informal register awareness

📝 GRAMMAR:
- Passive voice
- All conditionals (0, 1, 2, 3)
- Relative clauses including non-defining
- Reported speech
- Complex connectors: nevertheless, consequently, provided that

📏 SENTENCES:
- Complex structures with multiple clauses
- Varied length (10-25 words)
- Rhetorical variety

📖 CONTENT:
- Complex arguments
- Nuanced opinions
- Hypothetical scenarios
- Critical analysis

📐 LENGTH: 1000-1500 words
📃 PARAGRAPHS: 4-6 varied sentences
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq level "C1")}}
📚 VOCABULARY:
- Advanced academic and literary vocabulary
- Precise word choice
- Nuanced synonyms
- Register flexibility

📝 GRAMMAR:
- Inversion (Not only... but also)
- Advanced passives
- Subjunctive mood
- Cleft sentences (It was X that...)
- Ellipsis and substitution

📏 SENTENCES:
- Sophisticated varied structures
- Strategic sentence length variation
- Rhetorical devices

📖 CONTENT:
- Abstract reasoning
- Implicit meanings
- Irony and understatement
- Academic discourse

📐 LENGTH: 1500-2000 words
📃 PARAGRAPHS: 5-7 sentences
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq level "C2")}}
📚 VOCABULARY:
- Near-native proficiency
- Subtle connotations
- Rare and archaic words where appropriate
- Full idiomatic mastery

📝 GRAMMAR:
- Full grammatical mastery
- Stylistic grammar choices
- All rhetorical structures
- Native-like flexibility

📏 SENTENCES:
- Masterful variation
- Prose rhythm awareness
- Literary techniques

📖 CONTENT:
- Sophisticated argumentation
- Cultural references
- Implicit meanings
- Stylistic choices

📐 LENGTH: 1800-2200 words
📃 PARAGRAPHS: 6-8 sentences
{{/if}}